{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Who shall survive when He sets [them] as El.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 106a) interpreted two interpretations in this — one may examine their words there. And it appears to me that the verse follows according to the introduction from the words of the Zohar, that the cause of Israel's exiles is to refine the holy sparks that are deposited there among the inhabitants of that land. And this is what they alluded to in the verse (Ecclesiastes 8:9): ״עֵת אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁלֹט הָאָדָם בָּאָדָם לְרַע לוֹ״ — meaning, לְרַע לוֹ, to the detriment of the ruler, for the good portion will be refined from him and the evil portion that exists as his counterpart in the one ruled will be completed in him. And we have already introduced many times that when the good portion is absent from a person, this is his destruction, for it is the thing that sustains and gives life, since the aspect of evil is called dead. And from now on the verse will be understood in this manner: because he preceded by mentioning the captivity of Assyria, the Holy Spirit informed of the act of the captivity of His holy people, who are exiled among the lands, and said: woe to the nations — <b>from His setting them as El</b> — meaning, the enemy will cause them distress through the placing of the people of El in their midst, for in every place where HaShem places Israel, they extract from them all the sparks of holiness that sustain them and give them life. And this is the meaning of the verse itself: ״עֵת אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁלֹט… לְרַע לוֹ״ — to the ruler."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:09.769744001Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}